# Backtest Standards

## Recent-period slices

- Strategy backtests and audits must include recent-period slices by default: last `1d`, `7d`, `1m`, `3m`, `6m`, and `1y`, unless the available dataset is shorter.
- Slice windows are anchored to the dataset end timestamp, not the wall-clock time when the script runs.
- Reports must state market, symbol, timeframe, UTC data range, fees, slippage, funding/carry assumptions, and whether the slices were used for selection or only for audit.
- Do not promote a strategy if any recent slice reveals execution impossibility, severe data-quality issues, lookahead, stale stop fills, or a live runner mismatch, even if full-period metrics look good.

## Execution costs

- Binance strategy backtests must include execution costs by default: trading fee `0.001` of filled notional per fill and `4 bps` adverse slippage per fill, unless the user or a research document explicitly specifies a different Binance cost model.
- Do not apply these Binance defaults to stocks, A-shares, Hong Kong equities, commodities, or other non-Binance markets. Use the explicit cost model from the user or research document; if none is available, state that execution costs are unspecified for that market.
- Reports and decision logs should state the market, fee, and slippage assumptions next to backtest results so performance is not summarized without execution costs.
